// The exhaustiveness RATCHET for tests/exhaustive.zig. Read that file's header.
//
// One line per unannotated `else =>` over a boundary enum that existed when
// the guard landed: `<file> <enclosing fn> <enum>`, repeated once per site in
// that function. Keyed by function and never by line, so moving code within or
// between the files of a split directory does not churn it. Sorted (LC_ALL=C).
//
// The test fails on a site NOT listed here and on a line here whose site is
// gone. This list only ever SHRINKS: never add a line. A new site gets its
// prongs written out, or `// else: <reason>` on its else line.
//
// Shrinking it one site at a time is the slow way. The stage-boundary
// exhaustiveness audit (run at bb2acd8; not checked in), section 4.2
// "STRUCTURAL TOTALS", names four changes that each delete a class of lines:
//   1. Ast.ExprStore.children(): one exhaustive switch stating every node's
//      child edges, so the ~10 expression walkers stop copying them by hand.
//      A sibling stmtWrites() does the same for the lvalues a statement writes.
//   2. std.EnumArray(Mir.Opcode, OpInfo), the pattern op.zig already uses,
//      replacing the opClass/opIsInteger/predicate switches with columns.
//   3. One constant folder shared by elaborate, proof, lower, analysis,
//      codegen and sim, replacing six of the seven that disagree today.
//   4. A Callee enum for MIR calls, turning `mem.eql` name dispatch into
//      switches this guard can then see.
